import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Why is my PDF still over 200KB after compressing?', a: 'It probably contains many pages or very detailed images. Try removing pages you don’t need, splitting the file, or letting the 200KB target rasterize pages as a last resort.' },
  { q: 'Will compressing to 200KB make text blurry?', a: 'Usually not. Text in a normal PDF is stored as text, not pixels, so it stays sharp. Only scanned pages, which are images, lose some sharpness.' },
  { q: 'Is 200KB the same as 0.2MB?', a: 'Close enough for upload forms. Strictly, 200KB is 204,800 bytes (or 200,000 on some sites). PDFNU aims for 200 × 1024 bytes, which satisfies both.' },
];
---
<GuideLayout slug="compress-pdf-to-200kb" faqs={faqs}>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> open <a href="/compress-pdf?target=200">Compress PDF</a>, tap the <strong>200KB</strong> chip and press compress. PDFNU tries progressively stronger settings and stops at the best quality that fits under 200KB, so text stays sharp and selectable in most documents.</p>
  <p>The rest of this guide explains what makes a PDF big, how to get under 200KB when the first try isn’t enough, and how to keep it readable.</p>

  <h2>What makes a PDF bigger than 200KB?</h2>
  <p>Text is tiny. A page of plain text is only a few kilobytes. What pushes a PDF past 200KB is almost always one of these:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Scanned pages or phone photos.</strong> Each page is a full image, often 300–600KB on its own.</li>
    <li><strong>High-resolution pictures</strong> pasted into Word or PowerPoint before exporting.</li>
    <li><strong>Embedded fonts</strong>, especially decorative or Asian fonts that aren’t subset.</li>
    <li><strong>Many pages.</strong> Twenty light pages can still add up.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Compression works by re-encoding images at a lower resolution and quality, and by tidying the PDF’s structure. So a two-page scan can usually reach 200KB comfortably, while a 40-page scanned book probably can’t.</p>

  <h2>Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Go to <a href="/compress-pdf?target=200">Compress PDF</a>. The 200KB target is pre-selected from this link.</li>
    <li>Select your PDF. Nothing is uploaded; the file stays on your device.</li>
    <li>Press <strong>Compress to under 200KB</strong>. PDFNU runs a lossless pass first, then image compression at about 150, 110 and 72 dpi, stopping as soon as the file fits.</li>
    <li>Check the result card. It shows before and after sizes. If it says pages were converted to images, the text is no longer selectable but still readable.</li>
    <li>Download. The file keeps its original name, so it’s ready to upload.</li>
  </ol>

  <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="guide-article" />

  <h2>If it doesn’t fit</h2>
  <p>When PDFNU can’t reach 200KB without making the document unreadable, it tells you and gives you the smallest readable version. You then have three good options:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Remove pages you don’t need.</strong> Blank backs of scans, cover pages and instructions often aren’t required. Use <a href="/organize-pdf">Organize PDF</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Split the document.</strong> Many portals have one upload field per document type. Use <a href="/split-pdf">Split PDF</a> to separate them.</li>
    <li><strong>Re-scan smarter.</strong> Scan in grayscale at 150 dpi instead of color at 300 dpi. That alone can cut the size by 80%.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Keeping it readable</h2>
  <p>
    A PDF under 200KB is useless if the officer can’t read your marks or ID number. After compressing, zoom to 100% and check the smallest text.
    If it’s fuzzy, try the 500KB target and split the file instead, or crop the margins of your scans before converting them. Less empty paper means
    more of the budget goes to the content.
  </p>

  <h2>Why do it in the browser?</h2>
  <p>
    The PDFs people squeeze under 200KB are usually certificates, ID cards, bank statements and application forms. Those are exactly the files you
    shouldn’t upload to an unknown server. PDFNU compresses them locally with WebAssembly, so the document never leaves your phone or computer.
    Read more in <a href="/guides/are-online-pdf-tools-safe">is it safe to use online PDF tools?</a>
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
